.TH TULPAR-LIST 1 "2026" "tulpar 0.6.0" "Tulpar Manual"
.SH NAME
tulpar-list \- list installed packages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tulpar list
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ pattern ]
.br
.B tulpar l
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ pattern ]
.br
.B tulpar \-l
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ pattern ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Lists packages recorded in the package database, with their
installed version and hold state. When an optional
.I pattern
substring is provided, only installed packages whose name contains the
pattern are displayed. The filter options combine: a package is listed
only if it passes all of them.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dest " " \fIpath\fR
List the database of an alternate root.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-json
Print the package list as a JSON array instead of human-readable
text. See
.BR tulpar-json (7).
.TP
.B \-\-names
Print only the package names, one per line. Meant for scripts and shell
completion.
.TP
.B \-\-explicit
List only packages that were installed explicitly.
.TP
.B \-\-deps
List only packages that were installed as dependencies. Cannot be
combined with
.BR \-\-explicit .
.TP
.B \-\-held
List only held packages.
.TP
.B \-\-upgradable
List only packages for which a newer version is available in the
configured repositories, with the available version next to the
installed one. This queries the repositories once per installed
package and therefore needs network access; it finds nothing with
.BR \-\-offline .
In JSON output each entry gains an
.B available_version
field.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tulpar (1),
.BR tulpar-search (1),
.BR tulpar-info (1)
